#### EDUCATIONAL VALUE & AUSTRALIAN CURRICULUM CONNECTIONS

Rhythm Culture's African drumming and dance school incursions offer students an engaging, hands-on approach to learning through music, movement and cultural exploration. By actively participating in traditional West African music and dance, students develop practical skills while gaining an appreciation of different musical traditions and forms of cultural expression.

Rather than being passive observers, students become part of the learning experience. They explore rhythmic patterns, develop an awareness of tempo and musical structure, respond to instructions and cues, and work collaboratively to create group performances. These activities provide valuable opportunities for learning through participation, experimentation and creative expression.

Our workshops can complement learning outcomes within the **Australian Curriculum**, particularly The Arts: Music, while also providing opportunities to develop General Capabilities and support cross-curricular learning.

![](/media/2026/10/20230227_092315-scaled.jpg)

#### Music & The Arts

Through practical drumming, singing and dance activities, students investigate the building blocks of music, including rhythm, tempo, repetition, patterns and musical dynamics. They develop listening skills, explore performance techniques and discover how individual musical contributions combine to create a coordinated ensemble.

#### Intercultural Understanding

Students encounter the rich musical traditions of West Africa through traditional rhythms, instruments, songs and dance. Facilitators introduce the cultural background of the music, encouraging students to appreciate different artistic traditions, broaden their cultural perspectives and develop an understanding of music as an expression of community and cultural identity.

#### Personal & Social Capability

Group drumming and dance provide opportunities to practise important interpersonal skills. Students learn to listen to one another, take turns, follow musical direction and contribute to a shared outcome. Through these collaborative activities, they experience the importance of cooperation, participation and individual responsibility within a group.

#### Movement, Creativity & Expression

African dance and rhythm activities encourage students to explore movement, coordination, body awareness and creative expression. By responding to different rhythms, sounds and musical changes, students develop confidence in using movement as a form of communication and artistic expression.

#### Extending Learning Beyond the Workshop

The experience can also provide a starting point for further classroom exploration. Teachers may choose to build on the workshop through music composition, rhythm exercises, dance activities, cultural studies or discussions about West African musical traditions.

#### Programs can be adapted to suit different year levels, student groups and school requirements. We welcome conversations with teachers and curriculum coordinators about their learning objectives and how a Rhythm Culture incursion can complement their existing educational programs.

#### AFRICAN DRUMMING & AFRICAN DANCE SCHOOL INCURSIONS

Bring the excitement, energy and infectious rhythms of West Africa into your school with Rhythm Culture's interactive African drumming and dance incursions. Our workshops are packed with hands-on musical activities, lively rhythms and engaging movement experiences that get students involved from the very beginning.

#### African Drumming Workshops

Students are introduced to traditional West African rhythms and the excitement of playing authentic African drums as part of a group. Through rhythm games, call and response, drumming patterns and ensemble activities, they develop their listening skills, coordination, musical awareness and ability to work together.

From the first beat to the final rhythm, students experience the satisfaction of creating music collectively. No previous drumming experience is necessary, making our workshops accessible and enjoyable for students of all ages and abilities.

![](/media/2026/10/20230227_103025-scaled.jpg)

#### Encourage Diversity

Schools hosting annual events such as under 8's and Harmony Day  can incorporate drumming to encourage diversity within cultures, peers and friends and to learn about West African culture and music.

Every student will have a drum to play and all drums are real full sized West African drums. This ensures a beautiful sound and an authentic experience.

#### Just like in Africa, we use our voice

African drumming is an effective activity for encouraging communication and self-expression. Rhythms are spoken, sung and sounded out, bringing the voice and body together through music. Students often find African drumming accessible and are encouraged to participate with ease and curiosity.

Through call-and-response activities, students learn to listen, respond and communicate rhythmically with one another. Using their voices helps bring the rhythms to life while building confidence and encouraging creative expression. It also creates opportunities for students to contribute in their own way, without needing previous musical experience. Everyone has a voice, and every voice becomes part of the rhythm!

![school incursions](/media/2018/09/A08R7238-e1550205393645.jpg)![school incursions](/media/2018/09/A08R7271-e1550205581543.jpg)

![](/media/2026/10/20230227_105440-scaled.jpg)

#### African Dance Workshops

Our African dance incursions offer students an energetic and expressive introduction to traditional West African movement. Combining rhythmic footwork, body percussion, coordination and dynamic movement, students explore the relationship between music and dance while developing confidence and physical awareness.

Suitable for all year levels, these workshops are also a valuable addition to secondary school dance and performing arts programs. Students studying dance can explore traditional West African movement styles, consider the relationship between rhythm and physical expression, and draw on the experience to inform choreography, performance and practical assessment work.

#### An Engaging Experience for Every Student

Whether students are discovering African music and dance for the first time or extending their existing musical and dance knowledge, Rhythm Culture creates an inclusive environment where everyone can participate.

Through shared rhythm, movement and creativity, students experience the enjoyment of learning together, developing new skills and being part of something bigger than themselves.

#### Primary School & High Schools

Rhythm Culture African dance school incursions are for High School students and Primary School students that would like to further their multicultural dance studies and/or engage in a high energy activity that incorporates dance, rhythms, song and loads of fun .

#### Primary School Students

Primary school students can also enjoy the [Drum Sing Dance Program.](/drum-sing-dance-program/) Students will love the joy of West African drum and dance. Fun upbeat choreography that will inspire students creativity, release study pressure and create a sense of community within the cohort.

#### High School Students

High school incursions focus on delivering a dance that the students can then combine into their contemporary pieces for assessment. This can either be a one-off introduction to West African dance, or it can be delivered in a program with a series of sessions. Students can gain an understanding of African dance and its choreographic and performance-based elements.

West African dance differs dramatically from many dance styles taught in Australia, broadening students' knowledge of cultural dance styles. Students can use this knowledge in their final years of dance study curriculum to create an original dance piece combined with their contemporary dance elements.

#### Key elements in West African dance

_Stamina, endurance and focus are just a few of the key elements needed for West African dance._

Students will learn about dance in Africa. Traditional versus modern. Key elements and signature moves in select rhythms and the cultural significance for each rhythm. Dance facilitators will share their dance experiences in Africa and their love of West African dance. Dance workshops can also include live African drumming.

#### DRUM SING DANCE SCHOOL INCURSION

#### An Energetic Combination of African Drumming, Singing & Dance

Rhythm Culture's **Drum Sing Dance school incursion** brings together the excitement of African drumming, singing and dance in one lively, interactive experience. Students learn funky drumming and dance breaks based on traditional West African rhythms, combining music and movement in an upbeat, action-packed workshop.

Throughout the incursion, students alternate between playing the drums, learning dance movements and joining in with traditional songs. Each group has the opportunity to experience all three elements, before bringing their skills together to create an energetic musical and dance ensemble.

Designed for primary and secondary school students, Drum Sing Dance is fast-paced, engaging and adaptable to large groups across all age levels. It's a fantastic choice for Harmony Day, rewards days, end-of-year celebrations, multicultural events and whole-school activities.

#### TRADITIONAL WEST AFRICAN RHYTHMS – GUINEA, MALI & GHANA

What makes Rhythm Culture's Drum Sing Dance program unique is its connection to traditional West African music and culture. Rather than simply learning a collection of beats and dance movements, students are introduced to the foundations of traditional rhythms and the stories and cultural significance behind them.

Our experienced artists share music, rhythms, songs and dances from Guinea, Mali and Ghana in West Africa, giving students an opportunity to explore the rich musical traditions of these regions through direct participation.

![Vacation Care Programs](/media/2019/02/lakescollege2-e1549596785851.jpg)

#### A SHARED EXPERIENCE THROUGH MUSIC & MOVEMENT

Drum Sing Dance offers students of all year levels an opportunity to engage with West African culture in a fun, inclusive and memorable way. As students move between drumming, singing and dancing, they develop an appreciation of rhythm, coordination, musical expression and working together as a group.

Most importantly, the shared experience of making music and moving together creates opportunities for connection, self-expression and enjoyment. Students discover the energy and release that music and dance can bring, while experiencing the excitement of contributing to something they create together.

# How to Book a School Incursion

Organising a school incursion should be straightforward. We work with you to understand your requirements and plan a workshop that suits your students and your school.

**Step 1: Tell us about your school**

Let us know your school or organisation, location, preferred date and the age group participating.

**Step 2: Choose your workshop**

 Tell us whether you're interested in African drumming, African dance or our combined Drum Sing Dance program. If you're unsure which option would suit your students, we're happy to discuss the possibilities.

**Step 3: Plan your session**

We'll discuss the number of students, session arrangements, available space and the type of experience you're looking for. We can also discuss options for multiple groups or a larger school event. 

**Step 4: Request your quote**

 We'll provide information about pricing and the arrangements for your proposed booking. Costs depend on your requirements, so please include as much detail as possible in your enquiry.

**Step 5: Get ready for the workshop**

 We bring the instruments required for the agreed activity and work with your school to organise the session. You will need to provide chairs and the space.

[Quick & Easy Quote](/quick-quote/)

# Frequently Asked Questions About School Incursions

**What ages are your school drumming incursions suitable for?**

Our workshops can be tailored for primary and secondary school students. Let us know the year levels involved so we can discuss the most suitable format and activities.

**Does every student get a drum?**

 Yes. Rhythm Culture provides West African drums for students to use during incursions, with no additional drum-hire fee.

**Do students need previous musical experience?**

 No previous drumming or dance experience is required. Our facilitators guide students through the activities, making the workshops accessible to beginners.

**How many students can participate?**

 We can discuss workshops for individual classes, multiple groups and larger school events. Please include your expected student numbers in your enquiry so we can recommend an appropriate arrangement.

**How long does a workshop run?**

 Session length depends on the activity, age group and school requirements. Contact us to discuss the timing that would work best for your students and timetable.

**Do you offer both drumming and dance?**

 Yes. You can enquire about African drumming, African dance or our combined Drum Sing Dance program.

**Do you travel to schools across the Sunshine Coast?**

Rhythm Culture offers school incursions on the Sunshine Coast and works with schools in other areas. Include your school's location in your enquiry so we can confirm the arrangements for your booking.

**Can the workshop be tailored to our school event?**

We can discuss your event theme, student age groups and learning objectives to determine the most appropriate workshop format.

**How much does a school incursion cost?**

 Pricing depends on the workshop format and your school's requirements. Contact us with your preferred date, location, student numbers and the type of activity you're interested in, and we'll provide a quote.

**What space do we need?**

 The space required depends on the number of students and whether you've chosen drumming, dance or a combined workshop. Let us know what facilities are available at your school so we can discuss suitable arrangements.

